WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.640
Every Android developer knows the strict absolute rule

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:04.720
of the platform.

3
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.200
Never block the main UI thread.

4
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.160
If you need to parse a large JSON payload or process

5
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.240
an image, you push that heavy lifting

6
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.760
to a background thread pool, using tools like dispatchers.io

7
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.640
or dispatchers.default.

8
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.080
When moving to React Native, the architecture looks different.

9
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.120
Your JavaScript code, your components, state updates,

10
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.600
and business logic, executes on its own dedicated thread

11
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.080
separate from the native Android UI thread.

12
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.640
This separation creates a logical trap.

13
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.040
Because the JavaScript thread sits in the background

14
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.280
relative to the native UI, developers

15
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.480
assume heavy computation in JavaScript

16
00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.520
won't freeze the user interface.

17
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.080
But when you execute CPU heavy work on the JavaScript thread,

18
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.920
momentum stops dead.

19
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.000
This split-screen comparison shows exactly what happens

20
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:50.960
under load.

21
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.480
On the left, a native Android app pushes a complex calculation

22
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.040
to dispatchers.default.

23
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.400
And the frame rate holds steady at 60.

24
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.920
On the right, a React native UF effect

25
00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.320
hub runs that exact same math on the JavaScript thread

26
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.640
and the frame rate plummets to 10.

27
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.920
The React native app hasn't crashed.

28
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.760
Instead, the native rendering layer

29
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.480
is starving for instructions, causing scrolling

30
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.080
and interactions to lock up entirely.

31
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.920
To achieve 60 frames per second in React native,

32
00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.840
you have to discard the background thread assumption.

33
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.400
You must treat the JavaScript thread

34
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.000
with the exact same reverence as the native Android

35
00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.280
main thread and remap your understanding

36
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.080
of the rendering pipeline.

37
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.200
To map your existing Android mental model

38
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.520
onto React native's modern architecture,

39
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.120
we start at the base presentation layer.

40
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.200
This architecture chart shows the React native UI thread

41
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.760
mapping to dispatchers.main to draw native views.

42
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.680
The JavaScript thread access the control plane, dictating

43
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.120
the UI thread.

44
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.640
The new architecture introduces the shadow thread,

45
00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.040
running synchronous C++ yoga engines

46
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.840
to calculate layout geometries.

47
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.640
Together, these three interconnected threads

48
00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.320
replace the legacy single bridge model,

49
00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.480
giving React native a rendering hierarchy structurally

50
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.680
identical to native Android.

51
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.760
This introduces a paradox.

52
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.960
If the JavaScript thread and UI thread

53
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.240
operate in separate memory spaces,

54
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.640
why does blocking JavaScript freeze your native views?

55
00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.440
The answer lies in the strict mathematical reality

56
00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.040
of rendering at 60 frames per second.

57
00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.240
The device has roughly 16.6 milliseconds

58
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.960
to process and draw every single frame.

59
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.360
In a healthy render loop, JavaScript calculates changes,

60
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.280
passing instructions to the shadow thread

61
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.440
to update the UI inside the 16.6 millisecond window.

62
00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:40.280
However, heavy math makes JavaScript blow pass that boundary.

63
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.360
The UI thread receives absolutely no layout instructions

64
00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:44.760
before the deadline.

65
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.960
That empty gap on the UI thread is a dropped frame.

66
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.080
When this happens repeatedly, the user

67
00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.160
experiences severe stuttering.

68
00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.880
For a native developer, the equivalent mistake

69
00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.240
would be executing a synchronous network request directly

70
00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.160
on the Android main thread.

71
00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.840
You can spot this exact bottleneck in development

72
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.400
using the built-in performance monitor.

73
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.880
If your UI frames read 60, but your JS frames drop to 30,

74
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.400
JavaScript computation is choking your render pipeline.

75
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.000
This reveals the core temporal constraint.

76
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.880
While isolated by architecture, the JS thread

77
00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.840
is coupled to the UI thread.

78
00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.080
Starve one, starve the other.

79
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.960
In older versions of React Native,

80
00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.080
keeping this pipeline fast was difficult

81
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.240
because of a historical design flaw.

82
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.800
Every layout instruction had to be serialized into JSON

83
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.800
and passed asynchronously across the bridge.

84
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.880
This asynchronous hop created a measurable delay

85
00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.880
between a user touching the screen and the native view

86
00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.400
updating, resulting in disconnected interactions.

87
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.000
The legacy bottleneck forced every instruction

88
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.200
to squeeze through a narrow async JSON bridge,

89
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.120
delaying the render cycle.

90
00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.160
The new architecture replaces this with fabric.

91
00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.680
Now, the JS thread uses synchronous C++ calls

92
00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.560
straight into the shadow thread.

93
00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.800
The performance impact is immediate.

94
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.440
Layout calculations now runs synchronously in C++,

95
00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.240
matching the native Android view generation pipeline exactly.

96
00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.360
For developers, this enables capabilities

97
00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.280
that were previously impossible.

98
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.080
Callbacks like on layout now fire in the exact same frame,

99
00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:22.560
and scroll positions can be read without asynchronous delay.

100
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.000
You can finally execute synchronous measure calls

101
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.400
on view references directly by passing

102
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.000
years of complex async workarounds.

103
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.200
By running layout synchronously,

104
00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.920
fabric elevates React native to true native parity.

105
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.920
But this engine only works if the JavaScript thread is clear

106
00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.360
enough to feed it.

107
00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.160
When your app does drop frames, the primary tool

108
00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.800
for finding the exact location of the bottleneck

109
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.120
is the cis-trace frame timeline.

110
00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.160
In the cis-trace timeline interface,

111
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.400
look for the track labeled MQTJS.

112
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.480
A massive, unbroken block of execution time here,

113
00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:58.880
paired with an idle UI track below it,

114
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.400
is absolute proof of a JavaScript CTU bottleneck.

115
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.000
Once you locate the bottleneck,

116
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.800
you have three architectural strategies to clear it.

117
00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.240
First, reanimated workloads extract layout animations

118
00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.720
and gesture state out of your React code.

119
00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.960
They execute directly on the UI thread via JSI,

120
00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.480
bypassing the JavaScript thread entirely.

121
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.600
If the bottleneck is pure computation,

122
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.560
like parsing a massive data set,

123
00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.400
the second strategy is utilizing turbo modules.

124
00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.440
Turbo modules allow you to push heavy async operations

125
00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.480
down into native Kotlin thread pools.

126
00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.000
This instantly frees the JavaScript thread

127
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.200
to return to its 16 millisecond render loop.

128
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.920
Finally, if you are bottlenecked by heavy React state logic,

129
00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.480
you can manage the load using React 18's Start Transition API.

130
00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:46.960
Like handler.post-delayed in Android,

131
00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.240
start transition intentionally demotes

132
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.160
low priority state updates.

133
00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:53.640
This yields the thread, allowing the UI

134
00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.680
to process touches and urgent animations first.

135
00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.760
Raw hardware power cannot overcome

136
00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.560
a blocked JavaScript thread.

137
00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:04.040
Achieving native grade performance requires proactive profiling

138
00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.440
and explicitly offloading heavy work.

139
00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:09.720
React native's new architecture mirrors native rendering,

140
00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.920
but only if you enforce strict discipline

141
00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.400
over what executes on the JavaScript thread.

142
00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.520
Returning to our architecture diagram,

143
00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:19.080
heavy computation diverts from the JS thread

144
00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.040
into a native module thread pool,

145
00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:23.280
leaving it clear to ping the shadow thread.

146
00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.560
This isolation keeps the execution loop

147
00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.320
under 16 milliseconds, maintaining 60 frames per second.

148
00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:31.680
Mastering this thread allocation model

149
00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.280
is the absolute prerequisite for any advanced user interface

150
00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.280
optimization in React native.

151
00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.080
And you will need this model to tackle

152
00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:41.400
the final boss of mobile performance

153
00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.760
and the most common source of scroll jank,

154
00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:46.440
rendering infinitely scrolling data.

155
00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.840
The next step is to take everything we just covered

156
00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.960
about fabric and the JavaScript thread

157
00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.920
and apply it to flat list, the React native equivalent

158
00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:55.800
of Android's RecyclerView.

159
00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.800
By leveraging synchronous layout,

160
00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:00.280
we can optimize massive lists to achieve

161
00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:02.120
perfectly smooth scrolling.

